  m_Name: 
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  lander: {fileID: 443832401}
  opportunityRover: {fileID: 949386766}
  landingDuration: 4
  roverSizeMeters: 2.3
  legacyRockMaterial: {fileID: 2100000, guid: 08ffd663e2980aa3897a908b296aae56, type: 2}
  sampleSoundVolume: 0.18
  landingIntroductionText: Opportunity landed at Meridiani Planum in January 2004
    to investigate geological evidence of past water. Investigate each of the three
    sample stations, then choose what the evidence supports. These simplified readouts
    are educational examples, not live measurements.
  sampleNarrationText:
  - 'Hematite-rich spheres: Opportunity found these mineral concretions, nicknamed
    blueberries. Their setting and chemistry support formation through ancient groundwater.'
  - 'Jarosite detected: Opportunity''s mineral instrument identified this iron sulfate
    in rock. Jarosite forms in the presence of water, supporting a wet past.'
  - 'Red surface dust: color alone does not tell us whether liquid water was present.
    Scientists need mineral measurements and geological context.'
  incorrectConclusionText: 'Try again: these rocks tell us about water and geology.
    Opportunity did not discover living organisms.'
  conclusionNarrationText: 'Mission complete: evidence of ancient liquid water. Hematite
    concretions and jarosite, together with their geological setting, helped scientists
    reconstruct a wet past at Meridiani Planum. Red color alone would not establish
    this. These are simplified examples of real discoveries, not live mineral measurements.
    Hematite spheres are enlarged for visibility. Evidence of water is not proof
    of life. Opportunity operated from 2004 to 2018, far beyond its planned 90-sol
    mission. Contact was lost during a Mars-wide dust storm; NASA ended the mission
    in 2019.'
  landingIntroductionNarration: {fileID: 0}
  sampleNarrationClips:
